module IO/Stream

### A one-way byte flow read from the front only: a socket, pipe, or console end, or a {File} opened for
### sequential reading. Bytes go in at one end and come out at the other in the order they went in, so a stream
### has no length, no index and no seek. `pull` takes the next bytes and moves past them, and an empty result
### means end-of-stream (EOF). A source is the end you pull from, a {Sink} the end you push into. Places
### (memory, a box, an atomic) use `load` / `store` instead: reading a place again gives the same value.
###
### `Enterable` is ORTHOGONAL: a source that owns a closable OS resource (a file descriptor, a socket)
### additionally obeys {Enterable} so `using` closes it deterministically ({FileSource}); a source over
### a never-closed process-global stream (stdin) does not, and is used directly.
protocol Source
    ### Pulls up to `max` bytes from the source and moves past them. May return fewer than `max` bytes
    ### (a short read is normal); an empty `Bytes` (count 0) signals EOF. There is no length to query
    ### first — a stream's extent is unknown until it ends.
    ### :param max: The maximum number of bytes to pull.
    ### :returns: The bytes pulled, or an empty `Bytes` at EOF.
    routine Me.pull(max: U64) -> Bytes

### A byte sink you can only push into — a socket, pipe, or console end, or a {File} opened for
### sequential writing or appending. Like {Source} it has no length and no position; you just `push`
### bytes into the flow. As with `Source`, {Enterable} is orthogonal: a resource-owning sink ({FileSink})
### also obeys it so `using` flushes and closes; a process-global sink (stdout/stderr) does not.
protocol Sink
    ### Pushes `data` into the sink, returning the number of bytes accepted (may be fewer than
    ### `data.count()` — a short push is normal; retry the remainder).
    ### :param data: The bytes to push.
    ### :returns: The number of bytes accepted.
    routine Me.push(data: Bytes) -> U64

    ### Pushes any buffered bytes to the underlying medium.
    routine Me.flush()
